   "passage": "**Text 1**\nSchools that have banned mobile phones during lessons report calmer classrooms and more conversation at break time. This is hardly surprising. A phone on the desk invites constant checking, and even a silent one divides a student's attention. Teachers cannot compete with applications designed by experts to be irresistible. Removing the device removes the temptation, and students themselves often admit that they feel relieved. Whatever educational uses phones may have, they are outweighed by the hours of learning lost to distraction.\n\n**Text 2**\nA ban treats the phone as the enemy, when the real issue is how it is used. Outside school, students will live and work surrounded by screens, and nobody will lock their devices away. Learning to control one's own attention is a skill, and like any skill it must be practised. Schools that allow phones for clearly defined tasks, such as research, recording experiments and language practice, and require them to be put face down at other times are teaching exactly that. It is true that phones distract; that is a reason to teach self-control, not to postpone the lesson until adulthood.\n\n**Table: Survey of 400 students at one school after one term under each policy**\n\"I could concentrate in most lessons.\" — Ban term: 71% · Limited-use term: 58%\n\"I used a phone for a learning task at least once a week.\" — Ban term: 5% · Limited-use term: 63%\n\"I checked my phone secretly in class.\" — Ban term: 22% · Limited-use term: 35%\n\"I feel able to control my own phone use.\" — Ban term: 41% · Limited-use term: 49%",
